In article <34267229.FB39DA17@cinesite.co.uk>, Ana Pedraz <ana@cinesite.co.uk> writes:
>
>Has anyone got a clue of how to define a new pci scsi card on an
>Alpha2100 running OSF3.2c kernel?

This is documented in one of the system management guides, but
the short version is:

1.  Boot genvmunix.
1a.   save your current /vmunix and copy /genvmunix to /vmunix.  I'm
      not sure of this step as the build procedure may be smart enough
      to look at /genvmunix for its name list.
2.  Mount the local file system; run bcheckrc
3.  Run doconfig.
4.  Copy the new kernel to /vmunix.
5.  Reboot.

Doconfig won't remember what kernel options you previously chose,
so be sure to select the things you what when offered the chance.

If you happen to know the configuration file language and the name
of the used by the configuration for the new adapter you may be
able to add the new device to the configuration and rebuild by
hand.  The set of choices for PCI SCSI adapters is pretty limited:

psiop - KZPAA
pza   - KZPSA
isp   - KZPDA

You can look in the GENERIC configuration file to see how the
wildcards are used, so that you don't need detailed bus and
slot information.  For each of the previous three names that
might be added, you will also need a "scsi" entry.  Again, look
at the GENERIC configuration file.

