You're probably referring to DEC RAID card's limitations of 32GB per logical
RAID drive. BTW, did you know that those cards really are just Mylex DAC960's?
Nowadays, the newer Mylex cards have a firmware rev that kills that limitation
and also makes the card *tons* faster with separate block and line cache
parameters. Unfortunately, no, you can't just download that (freely available)
firmware into a DEC card since Mylex firmware rev 3.x (for dual firmware chip
cards) won't update a 2.x card (single firmware chip) like DEC. Here are still
two ways you could get around your problem:

1) Break down your storage space in chunks smaller than 32GB within
SWXCRMGR.EXE and assemble those chunks into one logical volume at the OS level
using LSM. I know it's kind of backwards, but (unelegantly) gets the job done.

2) Something I've never attempted but feel terribly tempted to do:
- get yourself a Mylex card of the newer kind. Set it up with all drives
attached to it in an Intel PCI machine running DOS in order to be able to
execute Mylex's utility program that allows setup of cards with this new
firmware. BTW, that program will flash the configuration in the card's nvram
*and* on each drive attached to it.
- pull the card out and move it to the DEC Alpha machine. At that point, the
card should just report a nice huge xcr0 drive to the console, and a nice huge
re0 to Digital UNIX. I haven't tried this but suspect it should work. Only
problem is that one must pull the card out to reconfigure it. Also, I have *no
clue* as to how the Digital Unix RAID management binaries (assuming you have a
copy of them - they come with the DEC RAID card but not the Mylex) will react
to this "foreign" card. It is not mandatory to run these management utilities
in order to have the RAID running under UNIX. It just makes it possible to do
stuff like rebuilding a failed drive within UNIX, i.e. *without* having to
down the machine and executing the swxcrmgr.exe program.

