In article <60cra5$qhg$1@news.nacamar.de>,
  hoevermann@dialup.nacamar.de wrote:
>
> Hello,
>
> As I'm not a UNIX expert, I've got a prolblem with killing a process.
> This process creates several child processes and starts processes over
> a 'system'-call in a C-program. Now I have to kill this process and
> all child processes, but if I'm killing the parent, the children are
> still alive! When I begin killing the children, the parent always
> spawns new children as long as I'm killing the previously existing
> ones, and I never come to an end.
> Can anybody help me?
>
> Thank you in advance
> Markus

Use the command kill with the TERM signal. For example:
To kill process 12345 and all children processes use
kill -TERM 12345  or kill -15 12345

(The signals are listed in the file /usr/include/signal.h)

You can also use the script below :

!/bin/ksh
#killall - To invoke type killall <pid>
#If action is OK uncoment the kill commnands
#Caution if it is running as root.
my_ppid=$1
#Obtain children processes
ps axo ppid,pid | awk '{
                         if ($1 == "'$my_ppid'")
                         printf("%s\n",$2)
                       }' | while read child_pid
do
   echo "kill -9 $child_pid"   # Display action
   #kill -9 $child_pid             # Kill child process
done
echo "kill -9 $my_ppid"       # Display action
#kill -9 $my_ppid                 # Kill parent process



Hope this helps.

Best Regards 8-)

Randolpho

