In article <60h296$mlf$1@nuke.csu.net> bkelley@ultrix6.cs.csubak.edu writes:
>  Basically here is the problem. I'm running a large database (manipulation/
>display/search engine) application on a DEC AlphaServer 1000/266 running OSF/1
>v3.2 with 256MB of physical memory, 574MB of swap, and a total of 8GB of disk
>space. Since this application is acccessed through a web gateway (httpd), a
>large number of simultaneous users may be running this program at once. The 
>AlphaServer is also being used as a DECNET file server. During heavy usage, say
>20-25 seperate instances of the database application running (20-25 processes),
>load average soars to roughly 25.00 to 30.00 (and possibly higher). I realize
>that this machine may be underpowered for the type of work it's doing (thinking
>of upgrading to 400Mhz processor), and that is a possible cause for the high
>load, but the real problem is an error message that the kernel is generating
>during this situation of high usage/load:
>
>	orion vmunix: fork/procdup: task_create failed. Code: 0x11

You have run out of process slots, and the system cannot fork another
process for you.

You should edit the kernel configuration file and up the "maxusers"
paramaeter from (probably) 32 to 1024, rebuild and reinstall the kernel.
If the processes are not running as root, you should also add maxuprc,
set to 99999999.

I think you can also do this from /etc/sysconfigtab, with:

	proc:
		max-proc-per-user=99999999


My memory of kernel tuning in V3.2 is a little rusty. If you need more
information, send me E-Mail.

