It is a dangerous thing to allow terminals outside the server console to be
able to login directly as root, as you will have no control over them.

If you absolutely must, then /etc/securettys contains a list of those
terminals which may log in as root.

It is better to make users log in as themselves, then su to root (or su -
which goes through root login).

If you want to see who is becoming superuser with su, and when they did it,
look for file /var/adm/sialog.   If it isn't there create it with  touch
/var/adm/sialog.

All attempts to become superuser with au are logged in this file, if it
exists.


Doug

