I have two boxes on the same network. One is a DEC Alpha running D.U.
V3.2d, the other is an HP Open View workstation. They behave differently on
receiving an ICMP redirect message.

The default gateway on both is set to point at the same router. When a
device issues a connect request via a different router, the following
occurs on the HP:

Let's assume the calling device is at 10.1.80.1. The HP sends the packet to
the default router, which sends an ICMP redirect pointing to the 2nd
router. The HP then adds a host route to the route table of 10.1.80, with
the
address of the 2nd router, through which the call came. Conversation takes
place, the connection is ended and after a minute or two the new route
disappears from the route table.

On the DEC box, something different happens:
The DEC sends the packet to the default router as before, but this time
when the router responds with a redirect to the 2nd router, the DEC box
*changes* the default router entry to point at the 2nd router, and stays
that way permanently.

It looks broken to me. Can anyone tell me if this behaviour can be changed
to add a host route? I am sure the RFCs don't allow such an action

I can get around the problem by adding a static route to the DEC, for the
host that is on the 2nd router (and shouldn't be calling anyway), but that
doesn't help when (if) another host connects through the second router. 

Thanks

Dave

