On Thu, 18 Sep 1997, Vasu Vallurupalli wrote:

> Here's the problem: The /usr/users/vasu dir on the w/s (client) is under
> the ownership of jim (as jim has the uid 218 on the server). Why doesn't
> the ownership belong to 200 (vasu) as the w/s has no account info. for
> jim and vasu, and the server shows that vasu has the uid 200?

Unix stores ownership based on uids not usernames, if there is an entry in
the passwd file, whether local or NIS, then it will show that instead of
the number when you list files.  So the solution for your problem is to
change the ownership to uid 200, you could change it to vasu but using 200
is better.  You can just substitute the number instead of the name, so the
command would look like this:

	chown -R 200 /usr/users/vasu

