Ana Pedraz <ana@cinesite.co.uk> writes:
>Hi,
>
>Has anyone got a clue of how to define a new pci scsi card on an
>Alpha2100 running OSF3.2c kernel?

First, what type of a SCSI controller card ?  Is it "supported" on your rev
of OSF (now DUNIX) ?  If the answer is "yes", find your "Guide to System
Administration" book and check the chapter on kernel configuration.

Basically, you will need to edit your system configuration file (in
/sys/conf).  Hopefully you will find some lines defining the other SCSI
controllers that were orginially in the system.  If they were on the PCI
bus (as opposed to being on the EISA bus) the config file lines will look
somthing like this:

<snipped from /sys/conf/SYSTEM_NAME>
       controller      scsi1      at isp0      slot  0
       bus             pci4000    at pci0      slot  9
       bus             isp1       at pci4000   slot  0 vector    ispintr
<more follows>

Then You need to define the disks on each SCSI bus.  You might as weel
define all possible disks and tapes on each SCSI bus.  It will look
something like this

<snipped from /sys/conf/SYSTEM_NAME>
       device disk     rz8        at scsi1     drive     64
       device disk     rz9        at scsi1     drive     72
       device disk     rz10       at scsi1     drive     80
       device disk     rz11       at scsi1     drive     88
       device disk     rz12       at scsi1     drive     96
       device disk     rz13       at scsi1     drive     104
       device disk     rz14       at scsi1     drive     112
       device tape     tz8        at scsi1     drive     64
       device tape     tz9        at scsi1     drive     72
       device tape     tz10       at scsi1     drive     80
       device tape     tz11       at scsi1     drive     88
       device tape     tz12       at scsi1     drive     96
       device tape     tz13       at scsi1     drive     104
       device tape     tz14       at scsi1     drive     112
<more follows>

Then just run 'doconfig -c SYSTEM_NAME'.  After the new kernel is built
move it to /vmunix.  This is a bit rough but it should get you going.



Jack Patteeuw

