Lets put this in perspective.  V2.0 was released on March 11th, 1994,
over 4 years ago.  That's just over 3 versions per year. Of those 3
(on average) one of those would have been designed to increase software 
functionality and the other two would have been bug fix roll ups and 
additional support for extra hardware as it appeared through out the
year.  

You have to appreciate that there are two forces at work here. On
one end of the rope there is a desire to provide stable platforms 
for customers to run their business on for a few years, without the
trauma of upsetting the apple cart with software changes.  And on
the other end, sales and marketing are yelling for faster hardware
and more features, so that when they're fighting for new business
they can, 1) meet all the requirements on the customer 'features'
tick list, and 2) be competitive with the competition.  Stuck in
the middle are engineering and manufacturing, who have to strike the
right balance between the two based on the resources at their disposal.

Doesn't this scenario apply to ALL computer vendors?
 
> Shouldn't Digital be aiming at quality, not quantity ?

We are. Of cause we are.  Why on earth would we not be aiming at
quality.  

John
