X-Deja-AN: 272591879
X-Sender: jjn@byron
Organization: University of Cambridge, England
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



Here's a cautionary tale, 

I have lots of Digital Unix machines backing up to a remote tape device
attached to a Sun workstation.

Everything looks fine and dandy, rdumps are going well no errors reported
from the DU boxes. On another cycle a Solaris box is backing up to the
same drive, and it gives errors.

Oh dear I think, check out the tape, fsck the disk, and am a bit puzzled
by this, as the DU backups are going fine.

Then comes one fatal day when I need to restore from one of these DU
dumps, nothing on the tape! Absolutely nothing written down, it turns out
that the tape drive was faulty and hadn't been writing anything for 6
weeks!

Digital unix was completely ignoring a write error from the tape drive and
reporting sucess on the dump.

I know that the moral of the story is to test read dumps more regularly,
but still DU shouldn't ignore the write error!

Regards,

Jonathan

