Organization: National Library of Canada
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



Hi all,

We're a shop that recently converted from VMS to Digital Unix. 

In the VMS world there was a facility called "TFF - Terminal Fallback
Facility"
that provided a table-driven character conversion for terminals. The
conversions
were transparent to the application.

This feature was used to accomodate users who could only connect using
VT100 as
terminal emulation (7 bit). Basically what TFF did was replace 8 bit
accented 
characters with the same character but without the accent. In this way
the output
from the application was still readable by the user albeit without
accented 
characters.

With Digital Unix what happens is that the high order bit of 8 bit
accented 
characters gets chopped off generating a totally different character. 

For example: the letter "e accented grave" becomes the letter i

Is there an easy way to duplicate the same facility in Digital Unix so
that it's
transparent to the application?

Any help with this matter is greatly appreciated.

Yvan.



