In article <341AA5A8.BFD0D312@ms3.hinet.net>, Vincent Huang <vincent9@ms3.hinet.net> writes:

>I didn't had any reference about how to use floppy disk, so,anybody can
>teach me how
>to mount a floppy disk?

It depends on what you put on it.  You can write directly to it
using any of the commands that are used for making tape archives;
tar, cpio, etc.  Simply use the character device file name with
the command:

% tar cf /dev/rfd0c my-directory

If you want need file system support, it depends on the file
system.  I believe a floppy is just big enough to make a UFS
on one, so you could simply:

# mount /dev/fd0c /mnt/wherever

If the file system is one created by a DOS system you can use
mtools to read it.  This is the set of commands include in the
MTOOLS subset which can read and write an MS-DOS format file
system.  The mtools manual page has a good overview of the suite
and how to set it up.

>I now want to reinstall DU4.0B in my system.
>I choose AdvFS option in my harddisk,but I found the file type still to
>be "4.2BSD" when
>I use "disklabel -r rz0c" ,after installation .
>How can I mkae my harddisk to AdvFS system by advfs command.?

If you have a 2nd disk you can use, it might be easiest to simply
create a domain for root, usr and var, create the file sets for
each and backup/restore each file system to the new disk.  When
starting on the new disk you need to use the option of disklabel
that writes the AdvFS boot blocks.  If not, then I think the easiest
way to clear the label so you can use AdvFS is to boot the system
from the CDROM and select the shell option.  That will let you run
disklabel to clear the label (disklabel -z).  Then you can continue
the installation and it should relabel and allow you select AdvFS
for the various file systems.
