David L Nicol <david@news.umkc.edu> writes:

>I am trying to share a directory from a DU 4 machine.  I
>have it mounted on the DU 3.2 machine, and I have directories
>exported from the 3.2 machine mounted on the Linux machine.

>The Linux machine cannot mount directories exported from the
>4.0 machine. This is what I get: 

># mount -a -t nfs
>rpc mount: RPC: Authentication error; why = Server rejected credential

Unfortunately, there are several ways to get that.  One of the
simplest is to issue the mount from a not-root account (mountd -n
bypasses that).  Some involve problems with the sender's IP address
and claimed hostname.  (Most of those checkes are enabled by mountd
options a, i, d, and s.)  I think I've heard of problems where the
client and servers /etc/hosts file didn't have matching entries.

Some of the errors log messags in /var/adm/syslog.dated/.../daemon.log.
