>> If anyone could shed any light on this it would be most helpful.
>
> I am using DEC Ada which allows you to tie a entry point (in a task) to
> a signal so I have no control over that.
>
>That could be a problem :-)
>
>Your description of the problem indicates that, under high load
>conditions, the requested SIGUSR2 is being delivered to the process at
>a point where the process' signal action for SIGUSR2 is set to SIG_DFL
>(which causes the process to terminate, and the shell then reports the
>reason for termination).
>
>Historically, this was almost invariably caused by the old behaviour
>of signal() (which still applies to some systems), whereby the action
>was reset to SIG_DFL prior to entry into the handling function. The
>ANSI C and POSIX standards still permit this behaviour, despite the
>fact that it introduces an unavoidable time window during which a
>second signal delivery will terminate the process. POSIX solved the
>problem by introducing sigaction() as a replacement for signal().
>
>If the DEC Ada runtime is using old-style signal handling to implement
>the facility to tie tasks to signals, then it is hopelessly broken.
>You would need to pursue this with whatever support you may have from
>DEC.
>

