Sang-

AdvFS uses vnodes (if I remember the term correctly) instead of inodes.
AdvFS does not reuse vnodes, so once an vnode address is used, and becomes
unavailable. So if you have a machine running a database that writes a lot
of tmp files, and your running AdvFS, you will run out of vnodes. At that
point you have to reformat the drive (after backing up your system, of
course). But AdvFS is a lot faster, and it is easier and dynamic to extend
partitions. 

The filesystem of choice, that I've heard, is to run the root partition as
UFS, and the rest of the filesystem as AdvFS. Oh yeah, avoid file
striping. Keeping the root partition UFS is for easier handling than AdvFS
in case of a file system problem like a head crash. I heard this
recommendation from two guys from DEC.

Hope this helped,

John

