skyang <skyang@klnet.co.kr> writes:
>I have a 2100 4/275.
>What is SCSI CAM
>Does it neccessary for configuring SCSI Devices?
>

SCSI (Small Computer System Interface) CAM (Common Access Method) is the
"protocol" that a Host uses to communicate with a Disk/Tape (or other SCSI
CAM device) on a SCSI bus.

SCSI is a "descendent" of SASI (can't remember what that FLA means).  The
early SCSI specification had some "vauge" (ie. unspecified) modes.  Years
ago, disk drive vendors used to sell their disks with different ROMS
depending upon which hardware/OS you were planning on hooking the up to
(Macs and SunOS boxes used to be unique).  CAM came out and was adopted as
the defcato standard before it was adopted and published as part of SCSI2.

It )CAM) is not "necessary" for "configuring" a SCSI device.  Prior to
DUNIX V4.0, as long as the device was built into the kernel (as it was in
genvmunix) then you could use disklabel and set the partition on the disk
and mount it with nothing else required.

Jack Patteeuw

