as the long battle goes on between which FS to use....  AdvFS won't be a
bad idea on the root partition because of a bug (feature) in OSF 4.0 where
if the UFS root FS is corrupt the system wont boot.  AdvFS will at least
keep the FS clean (somewhat) in case of an OS crash or something minor.  i
have seen this happen.  plus AdvFS can be an added benefit when doing
backups while systems is still in multi-user runlevel.  but if nobody
cares, then UFS will be the way to go.


John Goebel <goebel@mail.his.com> wrote in article
<Pine.BSI.3.95q.970907150420.6682A-100000@mail.his.com>...
> Sang-
> 
> AdvFS uses vnodes (if I remember the term correctly) instead of inodes.
> AdvFS does not reuse vnodes, so once an vnode address is used, and
becomes
> unavailable. So if you have a machine running a database that writes a
lot
> of tmp files, and your running AdvFS, you will run out of vnodes. At that
> point you have to reformat the drive (after backing up your system, of
> course). But AdvFS is a lot faster, and it is easier and dynamic to
extend
> partitions. 
> 
> The filesystem of choice, that I've heard, is to run the root partition
as
> UFS, and the rest of the filesystem as AdvFS. Oh yeah, avoid file
> striping. Keeping the root partition UFS is for easier handling than
AdvFS
> in case of a file system problem like a head crash. I heard this
> recommendation from two guys from DEC.
> 
> Hope this helped,
> 
> John




