It's not easy. Unix deliberately makes it so that passwd has to read from a
terminal, for security reasons. This is not very good for bulk user
additions.

On Digital Unix one way round this is this:
echo "$PASSWORD\n$PASSWORD\n" | rsh `hostname` passwd $USER

This of course assumes you have the correct /.rhosts file set up for root
rshs on your box.

This will not work on all UNIXs, however, it doesn't on AIX.

What we've done is write a couple of pages of C to pick a random alpha
numeric password and then use crypt to set this password in either
/etc/passwd or the enhanced security database. (This password is set to
expire on first login). Then output the username and password to a
temporary secure file. This is perfect for bulk user additions as the SA
can print off the password list and distribute it to the relevant manager.

If you want this code you can e me for it.

Regards
